 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \times W \times H)$ per frame, where $N$ is the number of layers, and $W \times H$ is the resolution [S1, unofficial]. Each layer requires a constant number of texture samples and arithmetic operations per pixel [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ for the input and output buffers [S1, unofficial]. The method requires ping-ponging buffers for light and view directions/colors across layers [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Performance is heavily dependent on the number of layers and the cost of texture sampling [S1, unofficial]. The use of compute shaders allows for parallel processing of pixels [S1, unofficial]. The 4-tap averaging and irradiance scaling add computational overhead but improve visual quality by smoothing light propagation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient handling of light transport in volumetric scenes by decoupling light and view rays [S1, unofficial]. The layer-based approach allows for easy integration of refraction and transfer functions [S1, unofficial]. The use of compute shaders provides flexibility in shader logic [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** The method assumes a regular stack of parallel planes, which may not fit all volumetric data structures [S1, unofficial]. The refraction gradient calculation is a simplification and may not accurately model complex light bending [S1, unofficial]. The background replacement is a hack for the last layer and may not be suitable for all scenes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Suitable for real-time rendering of volumetric data where light transport is important, such as clouds, smoke, or medical imaging [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Not suitable for scenes with complex geometry or non-planar layer structures [S1, unofficial]. Not ideal for offline rendering where higher accuracy is required [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: The method processes each layer once, with constant-time operations per pixel per layer. Total time is $O(N \cdot W \cdot H)$, where $N$ is the number of layers, and $W, H$ are image dimensions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Requires storage for multiple buffers (light direction, light color, view position, view direction, color, material) for each layer or ping-pong buffers for two layers [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Efficient screen-space approximation of light scattering and refraction; suitable for real-time rendering on GPUs via compute shaders [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Approximates light transport within the layer stack; may suffer from artifacts at layer boundaries or with large inter-layer distances; requires careful tuning of refraction gradients [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use**: Real-time volumetric rendering where approximate light transport effects are desired and full path tracing is too expensive [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use**: When physically accurate light transport is required, or when the volume has complex internal structures that are not well-approximated by planar layers [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method is a compute-shader-based volume rendering technique documented in the source repository [S1, unofficial]. The technique leverages Vulkan compute shaders for parallel processing [S1, unofficial]. The specific implementation details, including the layer-based propagation and refraction updates, are documented in the source code and associated notes [S1, unofficial].
+The method is described in the context of a Vulkan-based volume rendering implementation, specifically in the `vis-magnus-2018` repository [S1, unofficial]. It represents a compute-shader-based approach to layered volume rendering, focusing on propagating light and view rays across parallel planes [S1, unofficial].
 
